#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097447} :activity/id #uuid "cb0bf575-ae5d-5289-83a0-5fe4af2c7572" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048862 :topic/id #uuid "c7162eba-3656-5b62-8b28-391c01075d02" :topic/math-academy-id 1086} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097447 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097910 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to approximate the area $\\mathcal A$ bound by the curve $y= x^2+1$ and the lines $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. We can do this using a left Riemann sum with $n=4$ subintervals, as shown below:\n\n![](images/1b/1b25a722d5527db77cd7dd5fb581c0ffed9b9324913d390e2633211360b6a8ab.png)\n\nA better approximation can be found by taking more subintervals. For instance, taking $n=8$ subintervals gives:\n\n![](images/17/17f6c8e458b2e6fbd887728cbbcc7cb64a670598256f76cad69d9e5a9c3cf7b5.png)\n\nAs we continue to increase $n,$ our approximation gets better and better. In fact, we can get an *exact* value for the area by taking the limit of the Riemann sum as $n\\to\\infty.$\n\nLet's write down that limit. First, we need to express the left Riemann sum using sigma notation. Using $n$ subintervals between the endpoints $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we express\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx {\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(0+k\\cdot Δx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(k\\cdot Δx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}({(k\\cdot Δx)}^{2}+1)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}({k}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}(\\frac{{k}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we used the fact that $x_k = 0 + k\\Delta x = k\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1 - 0}{n} = \\dfrac 1 n,$ as we've seen before.\n\nThe exact area is found by computing the limit as $n\\to\\infty.$ So instead of using an approximation symbol $(\\approx),$ we can now use an equality symbol and write\n\n$$\nA = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left(\\dfrac{k^2}{n^2} + 1\\right)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nThe formula above is a bit clunky, so instead of writing it using limits and sigma notation, we introduce a new notation called the **definite integral**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left(\\dfrac{k^2}{n^2} + 1\\right)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n}= \\int_0^1 (x^2 + 1)\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our example, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 (x^2+1)\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\ncan be interpreted as the exact area under the graph of $y = f(x) = x^2+1$ between $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis." :tutorial/id #uuid "10c42cfd-895e-5844-b943-efc5be835821" :tutorial/math-academy-id 759 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dbe8a28f-ae9c-5fce-a185-e8029413c272" :step/math-academy-id 1457 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097449}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097448 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096630 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096431 :question/id #uuid "e375ba82-b677-5e41-a81f-cea18470ab4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-405"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050517}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319069}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"} :step/id #uuid "9c8a3595-093e-5ea9-b728-e20ce01d63e5" :step/math-academy-id 1458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097450}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097449 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097911 :tutorial/content "In general, for a function $f(x)$ defined on $[a,b],$ the **definite integral** of $f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be defined using the limit of a left  Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nThe limit $x=a$ is referred to as the **lower limit**, whereas $x=b$ is called the **upper limit**." :tutorial/id #uuid "9ef3b0cc-ce87-5a51-9a34-0e45b0a342d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 761 :tutorial/title "General Notation"} :step/id #uuid "b767cb65-6bfb-54c1-b5e9-034fb71e87da" :step/math-academy-id 1461 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097448}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097450 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096631 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096432 :question/id #uuid "cd5b3d07-472e-50cc-b584-569feb90f927" :question/math-academy-id "e-406"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048988}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393257}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"} :step/id #uuid "1ed0244b-1489-5e87-9ec9-dd2f6566ad85" :step/math-academy-id 1462 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097451}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097451 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096632 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096433 :question/id #uuid "f7aaebcf-0a91-510e-8f09-513774affd6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-407"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396680}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"} :step/id #uuid "dfbce95d-2f31-5252-bde7-c60a98b5fb17" :step/math-academy-id 1464 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097914 :tutorial/content "There's nothing special about using a left Riemann sum to define a definite integral. A definite integral can also be defined as the limit of a right Riemann sum,\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k)\\cdot\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_k = a + k\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x =  \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nNothing has changed, except that the index starts from $k=1$ instead of $k=0$, and goes up to $k=n.$ \n\n**Note:** In the limit as $n \\to \\infty,$ the left and right Riemann sums give *the exact same result*!" :tutorial/id #uuid "c1ade2ee-0ed4-5dad-b2fc-a3a517dd3f1d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 764 :tutorial/title "Using a Right Riemann Sum"} :step/id #uuid "1cf484cc-b168-5f89-a236-0169328a1312" :step/math-academy-id 1466 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097453}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096633 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096434 :question/id #uuid "b21e2c30-fe7d-5f6b-ade2-7324be8d273a" :question/math-academy-id "e-408"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393271}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"} :step/id #uuid "0e12f8ef-911e-5e4b-85e0-2c32f12ef0b6" :step/math-academy-id 1467}] :activity/title "Defining Definite Integrals Using Left and Right Riemann Sums" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096875}]}
